1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,477
From orbit, in the middle of the Sahara, something is looking back at you.

2
00:00:05,477 --> 00:00:10,134
A giant eye, forty kilometres across, ring inside ring inside ring.

3
00:00:10,134 --> 00:00:15,000
It has caught astronauts' attention since the earliest space missions.

4
00:00:15,466 --> 00:00:16,798
It has many names.

5
00:00:16,798 --> 00:00:18,351
The Richat Structure.

6
00:00:18,351 --> 00:00:19,534
Guelb er Richât.

7
00:00:19,534 --> 00:00:20,866
The Eye of Africa.

8
00:00:20,866 --> 00:00:22,493
The Eye of the Sahara.

9
00:00:22,493 --> 00:00:25,600
And for some people, it has one more name.

10
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:26,703
Atlantis.

11
00:00:26,703 --> 00:00:33,004
The lost city of Plato, a capital built in rings of land and water, swallowed by the sea.

12
00:00:33,004 --> 00:00:40,366
Since a viral video in twenty eighteen, millions have been told that it was here, hidden in plain sight.

13
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:42,055
Others go further.

14
00:00:42,055 --> 00:00:49,933
An eye this perfect, they say, must be a beacon, or a landing mark, or the scar of something that came from the sky.

15
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:54,033
So today we do what Plato's sailors would have done.

16
00:00:54,033 --> 00:00:57,946
We enter the rings from the outside, one ring at a time.

17
00:00:57,946 --> 00:00:59,973
Seven rings, seven questions.

18
00:00:59,973 --> 00:01:01,999
And at the core, the verdict.

19
00:01:06,466 --> 00:01:07,983
Ring one: the orbit.

20
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:18,066
The Eye sits on the Adrar plateau of Mauritania, near the old caravan town of Ouadane, about twenty-one degrees north of the equator.

21
00:01:18,533 --> 00:01:23,270
Measured across its outer rings it is about forty kilometres wide.

22
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:25,352
NASA puts it closer to fifty.

23
00:01:25,352 --> 00:01:28,582
Either way, you can't see it from the ground.

24
00:01:28,582 --> 00:01:32,099
Standing inside it, you only see ridges and sand.

25
00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:35,055
From the air, the pattern is clear.

26
00:01:35,055 --> 00:01:40,957
High rings of hard rock, separated by low valleys of softer rock and drifting sand.

27
00:01:40,957 --> 00:01:46,433
The whole plateau stands about two hundred metres above the surrounding sand.

28
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:52,246
Those hard rings are made of quartzite, a sandstone so tough it barely erodes.

29
00:01:52,246 --> 00:01:57,866
Wind and water wear down everything around it, and the quartzite is left standing.

30
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:01,444
It isn't the only strange landmark here.

31
00:02:01,444 --> 00:02:09,300
To the northwest rises Kediet ej Jill, a mountain made of magnetite, so magnetic it upsets compasses.

32
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:12,333
This is a strange corner of the planet.

33
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:17,718
Geologists first described the Eye in the nineteen thirties and forties.

34
00:02:17,718 --> 00:02:19,972
Some called it the Richât crater.

35
00:02:19,972 --> 00:02:22,500
That word, crater, will matter later.

36
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:25,763
So the first ring holds a simple fact.

37
00:02:25,763 --> 00:02:29,222
The Eye is real, huge, and strikingly circular.

38
00:02:29,222 --> 00:02:32,166
Now, the story people laid on top of it.

39
00:02:32,633 --> 00:02:34,201
Ring two: the story.

40
00:02:34,201 --> 00:02:43,295
Atlantis appears in two dialogues by Plato, the Timaeus and the Critias, written around three hundred and sixty BCE.

41
00:02:43,295 --> 00:02:45,333
No older text mentions it.

42
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:51,600
In the story, the Athenian statesman Solon hears it from Egyptian priests at Sais.

43
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:58,533
Nine thousand years before his time, they say, a great sea power attacked Athens and was defeated.

44
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:06,054
That power lay out in the Atlantic Ocean, beyond the Pillars of Heracles, the Strait of Gibraltar.

45
00:03:06,054 --> 00:03:10,733
Plato calls it an island larger than Libya and Asia put together.

46
00:03:11,199 --> 00:03:16,251
At its heart, Poseidon enclosed a hill with rings of sea and land.

47
00:03:16,251 --> 00:03:21,226
Two of land, three of water, turned, Plato says, as with a lathe.

48
00:03:21,226 --> 00:03:24,900
Hot and cold springs rose on the central island.

49
00:03:25,366 --> 00:03:27,564
He adds detail upon detail.

50
00:03:27,564 --> 00:03:30,494
Walls of red, white and black stone.

51
00:03:30,494 --> 00:03:33,668
A canal from the sea fifty stadia long.

52
00:03:33,668 --> 00:03:35,215
Herds of elephants.

53
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:39,366
A vast plain sheltered from the north by mountains.

54
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:40,962
And then the end.

55
00:03:40,962 --> 00:03:48,933
After violent earthquakes and floods, in a single day and night, the island of Atlantis sank into the depths of the sea.

56
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:50,687
Notice two things.

57
00:03:50,687 --> 00:03:54,120
The Critias breaks off mid-sentence, unfinished.

58
00:03:54,120 --> 00:04:02,703
And most classical scholars read Atlantis as Plato's invention, a moral tale about a proud empire and a virtuous Athens.

59
00:04:02,703 --> 00:04:10,999
Even Plato's famous translator, Benjamin Jowett, wrote that no one knew better than Plato how to invent a noble lie.

60
00:04:11,466 --> 00:04:12,933
Ring three: the match.

61
00:04:12,933 --> 00:04:21,333
In September twenty eighteen, the YouTube channel Bright Insight posted a video arguing that the Richat Structure is Atlantis.

62
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:27,600
Within days it had hundreds of thousands of views, and tabloids around the world picked it up.

63
00:04:28,066 --> 00:04:29,985
The argument is a checklist.

64
00:04:29,985 --> 00:04:31,629
Concentric rings, check.

65
00:04:31,629 --> 00:04:36,220
Three of water and two of land, if you imagine the valleys flooded.

66
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:44,100
A diameter of a hundred and twenty-seven stadia, about twenty-three and a half kilometres, measured on Google Maps.

67
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:48,219
Mountains to the north, like Plato's sheltering range.

68
00:04:48,219 --> 00:04:49,775
A flat plain around it.

69
00:04:49,775 --> 00:04:53,766
A sandy channel that, it's claimed, once opened to the sea.

70
00:04:54,233 --> 00:04:56,696
Rocks that are red, white and black.

71
00:04:56,696 --> 00:04:57,928
Salty groundwater.

72
00:04:57,928 --> 00:05:02,033
And a Sahara that was once green, with lakes, and elephants.

73
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,500
Then the date.

74
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:13,435
Plato's nine thousand years before Solon lands near eleven thousand six hundred years ago, the end of a cold snap called the Younger Dryas.

75
00:05:13,435 --> 00:05:17,866
A catastrophe in the record, they say, that matches the flood.

76
00:05:18,333 --> 00:05:21,058
And on social media, the alien version.

77
00:05:21,058 --> 00:05:25,811
A perfect eye in the desert, visible from orbit, has to be a marker.

78
00:05:25,811 --> 00:05:28,466
Something built to be seen from above.

79
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:30,509
It's a persuasive list.

80
00:05:30,509 --> 00:05:37,635
But a list of matches only means something if the matches are precise, and if you also count the misses.

81
00:05:37,635 --> 00:05:38,800
So let's measure.

82
00:05:39,266 --> 00:05:46,400
Every claim in that checklist can be tested against two things: Plato's actual text, and the rock itself.

83
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:48,166
Let's start with the text.

84
00:05:48,633 --> 00:05:50,431
Ring four: the measure.

85
00:05:50,431 --> 00:05:52,385
Plato gives exact widths.

86
00:05:52,385 --> 00:05:55,433
The central island, five stadia across.

87
00:05:55,433 --> 00:06:02,000
Then a ring of water one stadium wide, land two, water two, land three, water three.

88
00:06:02,466 --> 00:06:07,412
Add that up and the whole ring system is twenty-seven stadia across.

89
00:06:07,412 --> 00:06:09,012
About five kilometres.

90
00:06:09,012 --> 00:06:14,466
That's Plato's Atlantis: a ringed citadel you could walk across in an hour.

91
00:06:14,933 --> 00:06:18,440
So where does a hundred and twenty-seven come from?

92
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:22,360
Plato places an outer wall fifty stadia beyond the rings.

93
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:27,174
Twenty-seven plus fifty on each side makes a hundred and twenty-seven.

94
00:06:27,174 --> 00:06:30,199
That number is the city wall, not the rings.

95
00:06:30,666 --> 00:06:31,462
Now the Eye.

96
00:06:31,462 --> 00:06:36,101
Its rings run about forty kilometres, eight times Plato's ring system.

97
00:06:36,101 --> 00:06:42,199
To get twenty-three and a half, you have to pick one inner ridge and call it the outer wall.

98
00:06:42,666 --> 00:06:45,206
And the stadium itself wasn't fixed.

99
00:06:45,206 --> 00:06:54,871
Depending on which Greek or Egyptian unit you choose, a hundred and twenty-seven stadia runs from about twenty to twenty-four kilometres.

100
00:06:54,871 --> 00:06:59,033
With that much freedom, almost any ring can be made to fit.

101
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,734
The rings don't cooperate either.

102
00:07:01,734 --> 00:07:03,901
They are not clean and complete.

103
00:07:03,901 --> 00:07:07,896
Count the valleys one way and you get three rings of water.

104
00:07:07,896 --> 00:07:10,266
Count another way and you get four.

105
00:07:10,733 --> 00:07:12,424
And the canal is missing.

106
00:07:12,424 --> 00:07:22,033
Plato's most distinctive engineering, a channel three hundred feet wide cut straight from the sea through every ring, has no trace in the Eye.

107
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:25,742
So the measure holds one real match: circles.

108
00:07:25,742 --> 00:07:30,066
The numbers that sound precise turn out to be chosen to fit.

109
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:31,838
Ring five: the rock.

110
00:07:31,838 --> 00:07:35,361
If someone carved these rings, the rock should say so.

111
00:07:35,361 --> 00:07:36,666
So what does it say?

112
00:07:37,133 --> 00:07:37,955
It's a dome.

113
00:07:37,955 --> 00:07:43,781
Deep underground, a body of molten rock pushed up the layers above it like a blister.

114
00:07:43,781 --> 00:07:50,566
Over many millions of years, erosion sliced the top off, and the layers were exposed in a bullseye.

115
00:07:51,033 --> 00:07:59,407
That is why the oldest rocks lie at the centre and younger ones toward the edge, all tilted outward at ten to twenty degrees.

116
00:07:59,407 --> 00:08:04,699
That pattern is exactly what a dome leaves behind, and not what builders leave.

117
00:08:05,166 --> 00:08:13,376
Inside the Eye, geologists mapped volcanic rocks: rhyolite, rings of gabbro, carbonatite and even kimberlite.

118
00:08:13,376 --> 00:08:19,100
Two great rings of gabbro circle the centre, three and eight kilometres out.

119
00:08:19,566 --> 00:08:21,517
And these rocks can be dated.

120
00:08:21,517 --> 00:08:27,234
The carbonatites cooled between ninety-four and a hundred and four million years ago.

121
00:08:27,234 --> 00:08:30,059
The kimberlite, about ninety-nine million.

122
00:08:30,059 --> 00:08:34,700
The hot fluids that shattered the centre, about ninety-eight million.

123
00:08:35,166 --> 00:08:37,080
What about the cosmic scar?

124
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:41,260
Early on, scientists themselves suspected an impact crater.

125
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:44,733
It was a reasonable guess for something so round.

126
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:50,113
But impacts leave fingerprints: shocked quartz, high-pressure minerals.

127
00:08:50,113 --> 00:08:53,573
In nineteen sixty-nine, researchers reported none.

128
00:08:53,573 --> 00:08:59,733
A mineral first taken for coesite, the shock signature, turned out to be ordinary barite.

129
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:08,424
Since then, the work of Guillaume Matton and colleagues has explained the Eye as an uplifted, eroded dome over a magma body.

130
00:09:08,424 --> 00:09:14,593
In twenty twenty-two it was named one of the world's first hundred geological heritage sites.

131
00:09:14,593 --> 00:09:19,833
The rock's answer is clear: a hundred million years old, and made by the Earth.

132
00:09:20,299 --> 00:09:21,624
Ring six: the sea.

133
00:09:21,624 --> 00:09:27,436
Plato's Atlantis is an island in the Atlantic Ocean, and it sinks into the sea.

134
00:09:27,436 --> 00:09:30,600
The Eye sits hundreds of kilometres inland.

135
00:09:31,066 --> 00:09:36,374
Its outer rim rises about four hundred and eighty-five metres above sea level.

136
00:09:36,374 --> 00:09:40,933
To reach it, the ocean would have to climb nearly half a kilometre.

137
00:09:41,399 --> 00:09:46,414
And eleven thousand six hundred years ago, the sea was lower, not higher.

138
00:09:46,414 --> 00:09:52,802
Ice sheets still held huge volumes of water, so sea level stood tens of metres below today's.

139
00:09:52,802 --> 00:09:56,100
There was no Atlantic flood reaching the Sahara.

140
00:09:56,566 --> 00:09:59,228
But here the theory gets something right.

141
00:09:59,228 --> 00:10:01,045
The Sahara really was green.

142
00:10:01,045 --> 00:10:10,133
Sediments inside the Eye, with freshwater fossils, show lakes and streams there between about fifteen thousand and eight thousand years ago.

143
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:14,000
Elephants are carved into rocks in this region.

144
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:14,868
That's real.

145
00:10:14,868 --> 00:10:23,333
But the same rock art shows horsemen with javelins and chariots, much later scenes, far from any ice age catastrophe.

146
00:10:23,799 --> 00:10:26,286
The opening to the sea in the south?

147
00:10:26,286 --> 00:10:31,397
It's a tongue of sand blowing in from the southwest, more west than south.

148
00:10:31,397 --> 00:10:38,166
And the Younger Dryas was a long cold spell, mostly in the far north, not a flood in a single day.

149
00:10:38,633 --> 00:10:41,386
So the sea ring gives a mixed answer.

150
00:10:41,386 --> 00:10:42,948
A wetter Sahara, yes.

151
00:10:42,948 --> 00:10:46,966
An ocean, a sinking island and a one-day disaster, no.

152
00:10:47,433 --> 00:10:51,241
Which leaves the last ring, and the most direct test of all.

153
00:10:51,241 --> 00:10:54,033
If a city stood here, it left things behind.

154
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,250
Ring seven: the diggers.

155
00:10:56,250 --> 00:10:58,729
People really did live in the Eye.

156
00:10:58,729 --> 00:11:06,533
In nineteen seventy-four the naturalist Théodore Monod found remarkable concentrations of stone tools here.

157
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:15,148
Most are Acheulean hand axes, the long-lived tool kit of early humans, made from the Eye's own quartzite.

158
00:11:15,148 --> 00:11:17,166
Some tools are even older.

159
00:11:17,633 --> 00:11:27,391
Later come Aterian tools, made by modern humans between roughly a hundred and forty-five thousand and twenty-nine thousand years ago.

160
00:11:27,391 --> 00:11:30,399
Then a scatter of Neolithic spear points.

161
00:11:30,866 --> 00:11:39,683
Many of these lie on the surface because the wind has blown the sand away over many climate cycles, leaving heavier stones behind.

162
00:11:39,683 --> 00:11:46,533
Archaeologists found them mostly in the outer valleys, and generally absent from the innermost rings.

163
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,443
The ridges are lined with thousands of stone burial mounds.

164
00:11:51,443 --> 00:11:54,456
Most have never been excavated or dated.

165
00:11:54,456 --> 00:11:56,866
That is a genuine open question.

166
00:11:57,333 --> 00:12:00,183
And there is a real lost town nearby.

167
00:12:00,183 --> 00:12:06,499
Ouadane, a medieval caravan stop on the Saharan trade routes, now partly in ruins.

168
00:12:06,499 --> 00:12:10,966
Real, but younger than Plato's date by thousands of years.

169
00:12:11,433 --> 00:12:18,633
What has never been found: walls, harbours, canals, bronze, tin, orichalcum, pottery, streets.

170
00:12:18,633 --> 00:12:22,233
Not a single shard of the city Plato described.

171
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:23,843
Hand axes, yes.

172
00:12:23,843 --> 00:12:25,749
Hunters and herders, yes.

173
00:12:25,749 --> 00:12:27,274
An ocean empire, no.

174
00:12:27,274 --> 00:12:29,333
That brings us to the core.

175
00:12:29,799 --> 00:12:31,254
Seven rings crossed.

176
00:12:31,254 --> 00:12:32,200
Now the core.

177
00:12:32,666 --> 00:12:36,197
Concentric rings from an eroded dome: evidence.

178
00:12:36,197 --> 00:12:40,329
Rocks dated to about a hundred million years: evidence.

179
00:12:40,329 --> 00:12:42,958
Stone tools, but no city: evidence.

180
00:12:42,958 --> 00:12:47,766
A green Sahara with lakes and elephants: real, but not Atlantis.

181
00:12:48,233 --> 00:12:51,369
Atlantis built in the rings: no evidence.

182
00:12:51,369 --> 00:12:54,810
An alien beacon, or an impact scar: debunked.

183
00:12:54,810 --> 00:12:59,399
Who built the ridge-top burial mounds, and when: still open.

184
00:12:59,866 --> 00:13:00,738
The verdict.

185
00:13:00,738 --> 00:13:05,894
The Eye of the Sahara is not Atlantis, and it was not made by visitors.

186
00:13:05,894 --> 00:13:12,938
It's a dome of rock lifted by magma a hundred million years ago and carved by erosion into rings.

187
00:13:12,938 --> 00:13:16,133
The one real match with Plato is the circle.

188
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:20,518
Plato's Atlantis lived in a story about pride and power.

189
00:13:20,518 --> 00:13:27,166
Trying to pin it to a map means stretching the stadia, flooding a desert and ignoring the rock.

190
00:13:27,633 --> 00:13:30,204
And the real Eye doesn't need a myth.

191
00:13:30,204 --> 00:13:38,682
Hundreds of thousands of years of human tool-makers, a green Sahara, and a hundred-million-year-old window into the Earth.

192
00:13:38,682 --> 00:13:40,766
That's a story worth the trip.

193
00:13:41,233 --> 00:13:42,238
That's the core.

194
00:13:42,238 --> 00:13:44,123
Which ring surprised you most?

195
00:13:44,123 --> 00:13:49,399
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
